answer:
Programming is the process of writing instructions that a computer can execute. These instructions are written in a programming language such as Python, JavaScript, C, or Java, and are translated into machine code the CPU understands. Programming involves problem solving, logic, algorithms, and design. Programs can range from a few lines of script to millions of lines of code.

Example in Python:

  print("Hello, world!")

Example in JavaScript:

  console.log("Hello, world!");

Example in C:

  #include <stdio.h>
  int main(){ printf("Hello, world!\n"); return 0; }

Example in Java:

  public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] a){
      System.out.println("Hello, world!");
    }
  }

A variable is a named container that stores a value in memory. You give it a name, optionally a type, and assign a value. The value can change during execution.

Python (dynamic typing):
  x = 10
  name = "Alice"
  pi = 3.14159

JavaScript:
  let x = 10;
  const name = "Alice";
  var pi = 3.14159;

C (static typing):
  int x = 10;
  char name[] = "Alice";
  float pi = 3.14159f;

Java:
  int x = 10;
  String name = "Alice";
  double pi = 3.14159;

Go:
  x := 10
  var name string = "Alice"
  pi := 3.14159

A function is a reusable block of code that performs a task. It can take input (parameters) and return output (return value). Functions help avoid repetition and make code modular.

Python:
  def add(a, b):
      return a + b
  print(add(2, 3))   # 5

JavaScript:
  function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
  const add2 = (a, b) => a + b;

C:
  int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; }

Java:
  static int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; }

Go:
  func add(a int, b int) int { return a + b }

Rust:
  fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a + b }

A loop repeats a block of code while a condition is true or for each item in a collection. Common types: for, while, do-while.

Python:
  for i in range(5):
      print(i)
  n = 0
  while n < 5:
      n += 1

JavaScript:
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) console.log(i);
  let n = 0;
  while (n < 5) n++;

C:
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) printf("%d\n", i);

Go:
  for i := 0; i < 5; i++ { fmt.Println(i) }
  for n < 5 { n++ }

Rust:
  for i in 0..5 { println!("{}", i); }

A conditional runs different code depending on whether a condition is true or false. The main forms are if, else if, else, and switch/match.

Python:
  if x > 0:
      print("positive")
  elif x == 0:
      print("zero")
  else:
      print("negative")

JavaScript:
  if (x > 0) console.log("positive");
  else if (x === 0) console.log("zero");
  else console.log("negative");

C:
  if (x > 0) puts("positive");
  else if (x == 0) puts("zero");
  else puts("negative");

Go:
  if x > 0 { fmt.Println("positive") } else if x == 0 { fmt.Println("zero") } else { fmt.Println("negative") }

Rust:
  match x { n if n > 0 => println!("positive"), 0 => println!("zero"), _ => println!("negative") }

An array is a collection of elements stored in contiguous memory, accessed by index. Arrays have fixed or dynamic size depending on the language.

Python (lists are dynamic arrays):
  nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]
  nums.append(5)
  print(nums[0])   # 1

JavaScript:
  const nums = [1, 2, 3];
  nums.push(4);
  console.log(nums[0]);

C:
  int nums[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
  int first = nums[0];

Java:
  int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4};
  int first = nums[0];

Go:
  nums := []int{1, 2, 3}
  nums = append(nums, 4)

Rust:
  let nums = vec![1, 2, 3];
  let first = nums[0];

A linked list is a data structure made of nodes, where each node contains a value and a pointer to the next node. Unlike arrays, elements are not stored contiguously, so insertion and deletion are O(1) once you have the node, but access is O(n).

Python:
  class Node:
      def __init__(self, val):
          self.val = val
          self.next = None

  a = Node(1); b = Node(2); c = Node(3)
  a.next = b; b.next = c

C:
  struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; };
  struct Node *a = malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
  a->val = 1; a->next = NULL;

Java:
  class Node {
    int val; Node next;
    Node(int v){ val = v; }
  }

Variants: singly linked, doubly linked, circular.

A stack is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) data structure. The main operations are push (add to top) and pop (remove from top). Stacks are used in function calls, undo systems, and expression evaluation.

Python (using a list):
  stack = []
  stack.append(1)   # push
  stack.append(2)
  top = stack.pop() # 2

JavaScript:
  const stack = [];
  stack.push(1);
  stack.push(2);
  const top = stack.pop();

C++ (STL):
  #include <stack>
  std::stack<int> s;
  s.push(1); s.push(2);
  int top = s.top(); s.pop();

Java:
  Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
  stack.push(1); stack.push(2);
  int top = stack.pop();

A queue is a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) data structure. The main operations are enqueue (add to back) and dequeue (remove from front). Queues are used in task scheduling, breadth-first search, and message passing.

Python (collections.deque):
  from collections import deque
  q = deque()
  q.append(1)
  q.append(2)
  first = q.popleft()  # 1

JavaScript:
  const q = [];
  q.push(1);
  q.push(2);
  const first = q.shift();

C++:
  #include <queue>
  std::queue<int> q;
  q.push(1); q.push(2);
  int first = q.front(); q.pop();

Java:
  Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
  q.add(1); q.add(2);
  int first = q.poll();

Variants: priority queue, circular queue, double-ended queue (deque).

A hash table stores key-value pairs using a hash function that converts keys into array indices. Average lookup, insert, and delete are O(1). Collisions are handled by chaining or open addressing.

Python (dict):
  d = {"alice": 30, "bob": 25}
  print(d["alice"])   # 30
  d["carol"] = 40

JavaScript (Map / object):
  const m = new Map();
  m.set("alice", 30);
  console.log(m.get("alice"));

Java (HashMap):
  Map<String,Integer> m = new HashMap<>();
  m.put("alice", 30);
  int age = m.get("alice");

Go:
  m := map[string]int{"alice": 30}
  m["bob"] = 25

Rust:
  use std::collections::HashMap;
  let mut m = HashMap::new();
  m.insert("alice", 30);

A binary tree is a hierarchical data structure where each node has at most two children (left and right). A binary search tree (BST) keeps smaller values on the left and larger values on the right, giving O(log n) search when balanced.

Python:
  class Node:
      def __init__(self, v):
          self.v = v; self.left = None; self.right = None

  def insert(root, v):
      if not root: return Node(v)
      if v < root.v: root.left = insert(root.left, v)
      else: root.right = insert(root.right, v)
      return root

Traversals:
  Inorder   (left, root, right)  -> sorted order in BST
  Preorder  (root, left, right)
  Postorder (left, right, root)
  Level-order (BFS)

Binary search finds a value in a sorted array by repeatedly halving the search range. Time complexity is O(log n).

Python:
  def binary_search(arr, target):
      lo, hi = 0, len(arr) - 1
      while lo <= hi:
          mid = (lo + hi) // 2
          if arr[mid] == target: return mid
          if arr[mid] < target: lo = mid + 1
          else: hi = mid - 1
      return -1

JavaScript:
  function binarySearch(arr, t){
    let lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1;
    while (lo <= hi){
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (arr[mid] === t) return mid;
      if (arr[mid] < t) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
  }

Quicksort is a divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm. It picks a pivot, partitions the array into smaller and larger elements, then recursively sorts each part. Average O(n log n), worst O(n^2).

Python:
  def quicksort(arr):
      if len(arr) <= 1: return arr
      pivot = arr[len(arr)//2]
      left  = [x for x in arr if x < pivot]
      mid   = [x for x in arr if x == pivot]
      right = [x for x in arr if x > pivot]
      return quicksort(left) + mid + quicksort(right)

In-place C:
  void quicksort(int *a, int lo, int hi){
      if (lo >= hi) return;
      int p = a[(lo + hi) / 2], i = lo, j = hi;
      while (i <= j){
          while (a[i] < p) i++;
          while (a[j] > p) j--;
          if (i <= j){ int t = a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t; i++; j--; }
      }
      quicksort(a, lo, j);
      quicksort(a, i, hi);
  }

Merge sort divides the array in half, sorts each half recursively, then merges them. It is stable and always O(n log n), but uses O(n) extra space.

Python:
  def merge_sort(arr):
      if len(arr) <= 1: return arr
      mid = len(arr) // 2
      left  = merge_sort(arr[:mid])
      right = merge_sort(arr[mid:])
      result, i, j = [], 0, 0
      while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
          if left[i] <= right[j]: result.append(left[i]); i += 1
          else: result.append(right[j]); j += 1
      result += left[i:] + right[j:]
      return result

JavaScript:
  const mergeSort = a => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a;
    const m = a.length >> 1;
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    return merge(L, R);
  };
  const merge = (L, R) => {
    const out = [];
    while (L.length && R.length) out.push(L[0] <= R[0] ? L.shift() : R.shift());
    return [...out, ...L, ...R];
  };

Recursion is when a function calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem. Every recursive function needs a base case to stop, and a recursive case that moves toward the base case.

Python factorial:
  def factorial(n):
      if n <= 1: return 1
      return n * factorial(n - 1)

Fibonacci:
  def fib(n):
      if n < 2: return n
      return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)

JavaScript:
  const fib = n => n < 2 ? n : fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);

Haskell:
  factorial 0 = 1
  factorial n = n * factorial (n - 1)

Risks: stack overflow if recursion is too deep. Many recursions can be rewritten iteratively.

Python is a high-level, interpreted, dynamically typed programming language created by Guido van Rossum in 1991. It emphasizes readable syntax and rapid development. Python is used for web development, data science, machine learning, automation, scripting, and education.

Example:
  def greet(name):
      return f"Hello, {name}!"

  class Dog:
      def __init__(self, name):
          self.name = name
      def bark(self):
          print(f"{self.name} says woof")

Key features: indentation-based blocks, dynamic typing, garbage collection, huge standard library, huge ecosystem (NumPy, pandas, Django, Flask, PyTorch).

JavaScript (JS) is a high-level, interpreted, dynamically typed programming language. It is the language of the web — the only language that runs natively in browsers. With Node.js, it also runs on servers.

Example:
  const greet = name => `Hello, ${name}!`;

  class Dog {
    constructor(name){ this.name = name; }
    bark(){ console.log(`${this.name} says woof`); }
  }

  // Async / await
  async function fetchData(url){
    const res = await fetch(url);
    return res.json();
  }

Ecosystem: React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, Express, Next.js, Deno, Bun, TypeScript.

TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing. It compiles to plain JavaScript and catches many errors at compile time. It was created by Microsoft and is widely used in large projects.

Example:
  interface User {
    id: number;
    name: string;
    email?: string;   // optional
  }

  function greet(user: User): string {
    return `Hello, ${user.name}!`;
  }

  const u: User = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
  console.log(greet(u));

Features: interfaces, generics, enums, tuples, union types, type inference, decorators. Runs through tsc (TypeScript compiler) or Babel/SWC.

Java is a class-based, object-oriented, statically typed programming language created by James Gosling at Sun Microsystems in 1995. Java code compiles to bytecode that runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM), making it platform-independent: 'write once, run anywhere.'

Example:
  public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
      System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
  }

  class Dog {
    private String name;
    public Dog(String name){ this.name = name; }
    public void bark(){ System.out.println(name + " says woof"); }
  }

Used in Android, enterprise backends (Spring), big data (Hadoop, Spark), and financial systems.

C is a general-purpose, procedural programming language created by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs in 1972. It is close to the hardware, has manual memory management, and is used for operating systems, embedded systems, and high-performance code.

Example:
  #include <stdio.h>

  int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; }

  int main(void){
      int result = add(2, 3);
      printf("Result: %d\n", result);
      return 0;
  }

Memory: malloc, free, pointers, structs.
C is the foundation of Linux, Windows kernel parts, and many languages (C++, Java, Python implementations).

C++ is a general-purpose language created by Bjarne Stroustrup in 1985 as an extension of C with object-oriented features. It supports procedural, object-oriented, and generic programming. C++ is used in games, operating systems, browsers, and high-performance software.

Example:
  #include <iostream>
  #include <vector>

  class Dog {
    public:
      std::string name;
      Dog(std::string n) : name(n) {}
      void bark(){ std::cout << name << " says woof\n"; }
  };

  int main(){
      Dog d("Rex");
      d.bark();
      std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};
      for (int x : v) std::cout << x << "\n";
  }

Features: templates, RAII, smart pointers, STL, move semantics, lambdas, constexpr.

C# (C-sharp) is a modern, object-oriented language created by Microsoft in 2000. It runs on the .NET runtime and is used for Windows apps, web (ASP.NET), games (Unity), and enterprise software.

Example:
  using System;

  class Dog {
      public string Name { get; set; }
      public Dog(string n){ Name = n; }
      public void Bark(){ Console.WriteLine($"{Name} says woof"); }
  }

  class Program {
      static void Main(){
          var d = new Dog("Rex");
          d.Bark();
      }
  }

Features: LINQ, async/await, properties, generics, records, pattern matching.

Go (Golang) is a statically typed, compiled language created at Google in 2009 by Robert Griesemer, Rob Pike, and Ken Thompson. It focuses on simplicity, fast compilation, and built-in concurrency (goroutines, channels).

Example:
  package main

  import "fmt"

  func add(a, b int) int { return a + b }

  func main(){
      fmt.Println(add(2, 3))

      // Goroutines
      done := make(chan bool)
      go func(){
          fmt.Println("from goroutine")
          done <- true
      }()
      <-done
  }

Used at Google, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, and many cloud services.

Rust is a systems programming language created by Graydon Hoare at Mozilla, first released in 2010. It guarantees memory safety without a garbage collector through its ownership and borrow checking rules. It has been voted 'most loved language' on Stack Overflow for years.

Example:
  fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a + b }

  struct Dog { name: String }
  impl Dog {
      fn bark(&self){ println!("{} says woof", self.name); }
  }

  fn main(){
      println!("{}", add(2, 3));
      let d = Dog { name: String::from("Rex") };
      d.bark();
  }

Used in Firefox, Linux kernel, WebAssembly, embedded, and CLI tools.

PHP (PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor) is a server-side scripting language designed for web development. Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994, it powers a huge portion of the web, including WordPress.

Example:
  <?php
  class Dog {
      public $name;
      function __construct($n){ $this->name = $n; }
      function bark(){ echo $this->name . " says woof\n"; }
  }

  $d = new Dog("Rex");
  $d->bark();
  ?>

Frameworks: Laravel, Symfony, CodeIgniter. Modern PHP (8.x) has typed properties, match expressions, and JIT compilation.

Swift is a fast, modern programming language created by Apple in 2014 for iOS, macOS, watchOS, and tvOS. It has safe defaults, optionals, and a clean syntax.

Example:
  struct Dog {
      let name: String
      func bark(){ print("\(name) says woof") }
  }

  let d = Dog(name: "Rex")
  d.bark()

  // Optionals
  var maybeName: String? = nil
  if let name = maybeName {
      print(name)
  }

Used for iPhone, iPad, Mac, Apple Watch, and Apple TV apps. SwiftUI and UIKit are the main UI frameworks.

Kotlin is a statically typed, modern language created by JetBrains in 2011. It runs on the JVM and is the preferred language for Android development. It is fully interoperable with Java.

Example:
  data class Dog(val name: String) {
      fun bark() = println("$name says woof")
  }

  fun main(){
      val d = Dog("Rex")
      d.bark()

      // Null safety
      val name: String? = null
      println(name?.length ?: 0)

      // Coroutines
      // launch { doSomethingAsync() }
  }

Also used for Android, backend (Ktor, Spring Boot), and multiplatform (KMP).

SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language for managing relational databases. It is used to create, read, update, and delete data, and to define schemas.

Example:
  CREATE TABLE users (
      id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
      name TEXT NOT NULL,
      email TEXT UNIQUE,
      created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
  );

  INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Alice', 'alice@example.com');

  SELECT id, name FROM users
  WHERE name LIKE 'A%'
  ORDER BY name ASC
  LIMIT 10;

  UPDATE users SET name = 'Alice B.' WHERE id = 1;
  DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 2;

  -- Join
  SELECT u.name, o.total
  FROM users u
  JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id;

Dialects: MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite, SQL Server, Oracle.

MongoDB is a document-oriented NoSQL database that stores data as BSON documents (JSON-like). It has a flexible schema and scales horizontally via sharding.

Example (Mongo shell / Compass):
  db.users.insertOne({
    name: "Alice",
    age: 30,
    tags: ["admin", "beta"]
  });

  db.users.find({ age: { $gt: 18 } });

  db.users.updateOne(
    { name: "Alice" },
    { $set: { age: 31 } }
  );

  db.users.deleteMany({ age: { $lt: 18 } });

Node.js driver:
  const { MongoClient } = require('mongodb');
  const client = new MongoClient('mongodb://localhost:27017');
  await client.connect();
  const users = client.db('app').collection('users');
  const docs = await users.find({ age: { $gt: 18 } }).toArray();

PostgreSQL is an advanced open-source relational database known for standards compliance, extensibility, and correctness. It supports ACID transactions, JSONB, full-text search, arrays, and many extensions (PostGIS, TimescaleDB).

Example:
  CREATE TABLE users (
      id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
      name TEXT NOT NULL,
      data JSONB
  );

  INSERT INTO users (name, data)
  VALUES ('Alice', '{"role": "admin"}'::jsonb);

  SELECT name, data->>'role' AS role FROM users;

  -- Full-text search
  SELECT * FROM posts
  WHERE to_tsvector('english', body) @@ to_tsquery('postgres & tutorial');

  -- Window function
  SELECT name, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) FROM users;

An index is a data structure (usually a B-tree) that speeds up lookups on a column at the cost of extra storage and slower writes. Use indexes on columns that are frequently searched, joined, or sorted.

SQL:
  CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users (email);
  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_username ON users (username);
  CREATE INDEX idx_orders_created ON orders (created_at DESC);

Composite index:
  CREATE INDEX idx_users_name_age ON users (name, age);

Rule of thumb: index columns in WHERE, JOIN ON, and ORDER BY. Do not index every column — writes get slower and storage grows.

GraphQL is a query language and runtime for APIs created by Facebook in 2012. Clients ask for exactly the data they need in a single request, and the server returns it in the same shape.

Example schema:
  type User {
    id: ID!
    name: String!
    posts: [Post!]!
  }
  type Post {
    id: ID!
    title: String!
    body: String!
  }
  type Query {
    user(id: ID!): User
    users: [User!]!
  }

Query:
  query {
    user(id: "1") {
      name
      posts { title }
    }
  }

Response:
  { "data": { "user": { "name": "Alice", "posts": [ {"title": "Hello"} ] } } }

GraphQL avoids over-fetching and under-fetching. Supports mutations, subscriptions, and introspection.

CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls how HTML looks: colors, fonts, spacing, layout, and responsiveness.

Example:
  body {
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    background: #f8f9fa;
    color: #222;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .card {
    background: white;
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 16px;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.1);
  }

  /* Flexbox */
  .row { display: flex; gap: 12px; }

  /* Grid */
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
  }

  /* Media query */
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .card { padding: 8px; }
  }

Modern CSS: variables, nesting, container queries, :has(), clamp(), color-mix().

React is a JavaScript library created by Facebook in 2013 for building user interfaces from reusable components. It uses a virtual DOM to efficiently update the real DOM.

Example (functional component with hooks):
  import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

  function Counter() {
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

    useEffect(() => {
      document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
    }, [count]);

    return (
      <div>
        <p>Count: {count}</p>
        <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+1</button>
      </div>
    );
  }

  export default Counter;

Concepts: components, props, state, hooks, context, refs, suspense. Ecosystem: Next.js, React Router, Redux, TanStack Query.

Node.js is a JavaScript runtime built on Chrome's V8 engine that lets you run JavaScript on the server. It is event-driven, non-blocking, and single-threaded with an event loop.

Example HTTP server:
  const http = require('http');

  const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello' }));
  });

  server.listen(3000, () => console.log('http://localhost:3000'));

Express example:
  const express = require('express');
  const app = express();
  app.use(express.json());

  app.get('/users', (req, res) => res.json([{ id: 1, name: 'Alice' }]));
  app.post('/users', (req, res) => res.status(201).json(req.body));

  app.listen(3000);

Uses: web servers, APIs, CLI tools, real-time apps (Socket.IO).

Object-oriented programming (OOP) organizes code around objects that combine data (attributes) and behavior (methods). The four pillars are encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.

Example in Python:
  class Animal:
      def __init__(self, name):
          self.name = name
      def speak(self):
          raise NotImplementedError

  class Dog(Animal):
      def speak(self):
          return f"{self.name} says woof"

  class Cat(Animal):
      def speak(self):
          return f"{self.name} says meow"

  for a in [Dog("Rex"), Cat("Mia")]:
      print(a.speak())   # polymorphism

Java:
  interface Animal { String speak(); }
  class Dog implements Animal {
      public String speak(){ return "woof"; }
  }

OOP languages: Java, C++, C#, Python, Ruby, Swift, Kotlin.

A unit test verifies a small piece of code (usually a single function or class) in isolation. Tests catch regressions and document expected behavior.

Python (pytest):
  # test_math.py
  from mymath import add

  def test_add_positive():
      assert add(2, 3) == 5

  def test_add_negative():
      assert add(-1, -1) == -2

JavaScript (Jest):
  const { add } = require('./math');

  test('adds numbers', () => {
    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
  });

Go:
  func TestAdd(t *testing.T){
      if add(2, 3) != 5 { t.Fail() }
  }

Pyramid: many unit tests, some integration tests, few end-to-end tests.

A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another value. Pointers allow direct memory access, dynamic allocation, and efficient data structures, but can cause bugs if misused.

C:
  int x = 42;
  int *p = &x;      // p holds address of x
  printf("%d\n", *p); // 42 (dereference)
  *p = 10;          // x is now 10

  int *arr = malloc(5 * sizeof(int));
  arr[0] = 1;
  free(arr);

C++ (smart pointers):
  auto p = std::make_unique<int>(42);
  std::cout << *p;

Go (no pointer arithmetic, has GC):
  x := 42
  p := &x
  fmt.Println(*p)   // 42

Rust (ownership + borrowing replaces raw pointers):
  let x = 42;
  let r = &x;
  println!("{}", r);

A memory leak happens when a program allocates memory and never frees it, so usage grows over time. In languages with garbage collection (Java, Python, Go, JS), leaks occur when references are kept alive unintentionally.

C leak example:
  void loop(){
      while (1){
          int *p = malloc(1000);  // never freed
          // use p
      }
  }

Fix:
  int *p = malloc(1000);
  // use p
  free(p);

Common leak causes:
  - Forgetting to free in C/C++
  - Growing global caches forever
  - Event listeners never removed in JS
  - Closures holding references in JS
  - Circular references with no weak pointers

Tools: valgrind (C/C++), heaptrack, Chrome DevTools memory profiler, Python tracemalloc.

WebAssembly (Wasm) is a binary instruction format that runs in modern browsers and other environments. It allows C, C++, Rust, Go, and other languages to run on the web at near-native speed.

Rust to WASM:
  // Cargo.toml
  // [lib]
  // crate-type = ["cdylib"]
  // [dependencies]
  // wasm-bindgen = "0.2"

  use wasm_bindgen::prelude::*;

  #[wasm_bindgen]
  pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a + b }

  wasm-pack build --target web

Use from JavaScript:
  import init, { add } from './pkg/my_lib.js';
  await init();
  console.log(add(2, 3));

Use cases: games, video editing, cryptography, CAD, scientific computing, porting native libraries to the browser.

A callback is a function passed to another function, to be called later when something happens. Callbacks are central to JavaScript and event-driven programming.

JavaScript:
  function fetchData(url, callback) {
    setTimeout(() => {
      callback(null, { id: 1, name: 'Alice' });
    }, 1000);
  }

  fetchData('/user', (err, data) => {
    if (err) return console.error(err);
    console.log(data);
  });

Callback hell:
  step1(a => step2(a, b => step3(b, c => step4(c, ...))))

Fixes: Promises, async/await.

Python:
  def greet(name, callback):
      print('Hello', name)
      callback()

  greet('Alice', lambda: print('Done'))

A Promise represents the eventual result of an asynchronous operation. It has three states: pending, fulfilled, rejected.

JavaScript:
  const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve(42), 1000);
  });

  p.then(value => console.log(value))   // 42
   .catch(err => console.error(err));

  // Chaining
  fetch('/user')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(user => console.log(user))
    .catch(err => console.error(err));

  // Parallel
  Promise.all([fetch('/a'), fetch('/b')])
    .then(([a, b]) => console.log(a, b));

  Promise.race([fetch('/a'), fetch('/b')])
    .then(winner => console.log(winner));

  Promise.allSettled([...]);
  Promise.any([...]);

async/await is syntactic sugar on top of Promises:
  async function load() {
    const r = await fetch('/user');
    return r.json();
  }

async/await lets you write asynchronous code that looks synchronous. 'await' pauses the async function until the promise resolves.

JavaScript:
  async function loadUser(id) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/users/${id}`);
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Not found');
      const user = await res.json();
      return user;
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(err);
    }
  }

  loadUser(1).then(u => console.log(u));

Python (asyncio):
  import asyncio, aiohttp

  async def fetch(url):
      async with aiohttp.ClientSession() as s:
          async with s.get(url) as r:
              return await r.text()

  async def main():
      html = await fetch('https://example.com')
      print(len(html))

  asyncio.run(main())

Rules: await only inside async; use try/catch for errors; run in parallel with Promise.all / asyncio.gather.

A closure is a function that remembers the variables from the scope in which it was created, even after that scope has returned.

JavaScript:
  function counter() {
    let count = 0;
    return function() {
      count++;
      return count;
    };
  }

  const c = counter();
  console.log(c());   // 1
  console.log(c());   // 2

Python:
  def counter():
      count = 0
      def inc():
          nonlocal count
          count += 1
          return count
      return inc

  c = counter()
  print(c())   # 1
  print(c())   # 2

Uses: private state, function factories, decorators, event handlers, partial application.

A load balancer distributes incoming network traffic across multiple servers to improve availability, reliability, and scalability.

Algorithms:
  - Round robin
  - Least connections
  - Weighted round robin
  - IP hash (sticky sessions)
  - Random

Layers:
  - Layer 4 (TCP): routes by IP and port
  - Layer 7 (HTTP): routes by URL, headers, cookies

Common tools: Nginx, HAProxy, Envoy, AWS ELB/ALB, Cloudflare, Traefik.

Nginx example:
  upstream app {
      server 10.0.0.1:3000;
      server 10.0.0.2:3000;
      server 10.0.0.3:3000 backup;
  }

  server {
      listen 80;
      location / {
          proxy_pass http://app;
      }
  }

Also useful: health checks, SSL termination, session persistence.

A reverse proxy sits in front of one or more servers and forwards client requests to them. It hides the backend, terminates TLS, caches, compresses, and can load balance.

Nginx reverse proxy example:
  server {
      listen 443 ssl;
      server_name example.com;

      ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/cert.pem;
      ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/key.pem;

      location /api/ {
          proxy_pass http://backend:8000/;
          proxy_set_header Host $host;
          proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
      }

      location / {
          root /var/www/html;
          try_files $uri /index.html;
      }
  }

Common reverse proxies: Nginx, Apache, Caddy, Traefik, HAProxy.

Difference from forward proxy: a forward proxy hides clients (VPN, corporate proxy); a reverse proxy hides servers.

A design system is a collection of reusable components, guidelines, and tokens that keeps UI consistent across a product.

Parts of a design system:
  - Design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, shadows)
  - Components (buttons, inputs, modals, tables)
  - Patterns (forms, navigation, layouts)
  - Documentation and usage rules
  - Accessibility guidance

Example CSS variables:
  :root {
    --color-primary: #3563e9;
    --color-bg: #ffffff;
    --color-fg: #12151c;
    --space-1: 4px;
    --space-2: 8px;
    --space-3: 16px;
    --radius-md: 8px;
    --font-body: system-ui, sans-serif;
  }

  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--color-primary);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

Examples: Material Design, Apple HIG, Radix, Chakra, Tailwind UI, Bootstrap.

HTTP is a request/response protocol: the client sends a request, the server responds. WebSocket is a full-duplex, persistent connection where both sides can send messages at any time.

HTTP:
  - One-shot exchanges
  - Stateless
  - Overhead per request (headers)
  - Great for REST APIs, static content

WebSocket:
  - Persistent connection (upgrade from HTTP)
  - Low-latency bidirectional
  - Small frame overhead
  - Ideal for chat, live updates, gaming, trading

JavaScript WebSocket:
  const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/chat');
  ws.onopen = () => ws.send('hello');
  ws.onmessage = e => console.log('server:', e.data);
  ws.onclose = () => console.log('closed');

Server (Node.js with ws):
  const { WebSocketServer } = require('ws');
  const wss = new WebSocketServer({ port: 8080 });
  wss.on('connection', ws => {
    ws.on('message', msg => ws.send('echo: ' + msg));
  });

Alternatives: Server-Sent Events (one-way), long polling.

An exception is an event that disrupts the normal flow of a program. Languages provide try/catch (or try/except) to handle them gracefully.

Python:
  try:
      result = 10 / 0
  except ZeroDivisionError as e:
      print('Cannot divide by zero:', e)
  except (ValueError, TypeError) as e:
      print('Bad input:', e)
  else:
      print('Success:', result)
  finally:
      print('Always runs')

JavaScript:
  try {
    const data = JSON.parse(input);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Parse failed:', err.message);
  } finally {
    cleanup();
  }

Throw your own:
  if (age < 0) throw new Error('Age cannot be negative');

Guidelines: catch only what you can handle, prefer specific exceptions, log with context, do not swallow silently.

A null pointer (or null reference) is a pointer that does not point to any valid object. Dereferencing it usually crashes the program.

C:
  int *p = NULL;
  *p = 5;      // undefined behavior / crash

Java:
  String s = null;
  s.length();  // NullPointerException

JavaScript:
  let o = null;
  o.x;         // TypeError

Kotlin and Swift avoid this with null safety:
  // Kotlin
  val name: String? = null
  println(name?.length ?: 0)   // safe call + elvis

  // Swift
  var name: String? = nil
  if let n = name { print(n.count) }

Rust has no null at all — use Option<T>:
  let x: Option<i32> = None;
  match x { Some(v) => println!("{}", v), None => println!("none") }

A class is a blueprint for creating objects. It defines attributes (data) and methods (behavior). An instance is a specific object built from the class.

Python:
  class Dog:
      species = 'Canis familiaris'   # class attribute

      def __init__(self, name, age):
          self.name = name            # instance attribute
          self.age = age

      def bark(self):
          return f'{self.name} says woof'

  d = Dog('Rex', 3)
  print(d.bark())
  print(Dog.species)

JavaScript:
  class Dog {
    constructor(name, age){ this.name = name; this.age = age; }
    bark(){ return `${this.name} says woof`; }
    static species = 'Canis familiaris';
  }

Java:
  class Dog {
    private String name;
    public Dog(String name){ this.name = name; }
    public String bark(){ return name + " says woof"; }
  }

Related: inheritance, encapsulation, polymorphism, abstraction.

Inheritance lets a class (subclass / child) reuse and extend the behavior of another class (superclass / parent). It supports code reuse and polymorphism.

Python:
  class Animal:
      def __init__(self, name): self.name = name
      def speak(self): raise NotImplementedError

  class Dog(Animal):
      def speak(self): return f'{self.name} says woof'

  class Cat(Animal):
      def speak(self): return f'{self.name} says meow'

  d = Dog('Rex')
  print(d.speak())

JavaScript:
  class Animal {
    constructor(n){ this.name = n; }
    speak(){ throw new Error('abstract'); }
  }
  class Dog extends Animal {
    speak(){ return `${this.name} says woof`; }
  }

Java:
  class Animal { void speak(){} }
  class Dog extends Animal { void speak(){ System.out.println("woof"); } }

Composition is often preferred over inheritance: 'has-a' beats 'is-a' when classes are not truly subtypes.

Polymorphism means 'many shapes' — the same method call can behave differently depending on the actual object type.

Python (duck typing + override):
  class Dog:
      def speak(self): return 'woof'

  class Cat:
      def speak(self): return 'meow'

  for a in [Dog(), Cat()]:
      print(a.speak())

Java (compile-time + runtime):
  class Animal { String speak(){ return "?"; } }
  class Dog extends Animal { String speak(){ return "woof"; } }

  Animal a = new Dog();
  System.out.println(a.speak());   // woof (runtime)

  // Overloading (compile time)
  int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; }
  double add(double a, double b){ return a + b; }

JavaScript:
  const obj = { toString(){ return 'custom'; } };
  console.log(`${obj}`);

Two forms: overriding (subclass replaces method) and overloading (same name, different parameters).

A constructor is a special method called when an object is created. It initializes the object's attributes.

Python:
  class Dog:
      def __init__(self, name, age):
          self.name = name
          self.age = age

  d = Dog('Rex', 3)

JavaScript:
  class Dog {
    constructor(name, age){
      this.name = name;
      this.age = age;
    }
  }
  const d = new Dog('Rex', 3);

Java:
  class Dog {
    String name; int age;
    Dog(String name, int age){ this.name = name; this.age = age; }
  }

C++:
  class Dog {
  public:
    std::string name; int age;
    Dog(std::string n, int a) : name(n), age(a) {}
  };

Rust uses a convention, not a keyword:
  struct Dog { name: String, age: u8 }
  impl Dog {
      fn new(name: &str, age: u8) -> Self {
          Dog { name: name.into(), age }
      }
  }

An interface defines a contract: a set of methods a class must implement, without specifying how. It supports decoupling and polymorphism.

Java:
  interface Shape {
      double area();
      double perimeter();
  }
  class Circle implements Shape {
      double r;
      Circle(double r){ this.r = r; }
      public double area(){ return Math.PI * r * r; }
      public double perimeter(){ return 2 * Math.PI * r; }
  }

TypeScript:
  interface Shape { area(): number; }
  class Circle implements Shape {
    constructor(private r: number){}
    area(){ return Math.PI * this.r ** 2; }
  }

Go (implicit interfaces):
  type Shape interface { Area() float64 }
  type Circle struct { R float64 }
  func (c Circle) Area() float64 { return math.Pi * c.R * c.R }
  // Circle automatically satisfies Shape

Rust uses traits:
  trait Shape { fn area(&self) -> f64; }
  impl Shape for Circle { fn area(&self) -> f64 { 3.14 * self.r * self.r } }

A static method belongs to the class, not to any instance. It does not receive 'self' (Python) or 'this' (Java/JS). Call it on the class itself.

Python:
  class Math:
      @staticmethod
      def add(a, b):
          return a + b

  Math.add(2, 3)      # 5
  Math().add(2, 3)    # also works

JavaScript:
  class Math {
    static add(a, b){ return a + b; }
  }
  Math.add(2, 3);

Java:
  class Math {
    static int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; }
  }
  Math.add(2, 3);

Go has package-level functions instead:
  package math
  func Add(a, b int) int { return a + b }

Use static methods for utility functions that do not need instance state. Avoid them for behavior that belongs on objects.

Generics let you write code that works with any type, while preserving type safety. Also called templates in C++.

TypeScript:
  function identity<T>(x: T): T { return x; }
  const n = identity<number>(5);
  const s = identity('hello');

  class Box<T> {
    constructor(public value: T) {}
  }
  const b = new Box<number>(42);

Java:
  public <T> T identity(T x) { return x; }
  List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

C++ templates:
  template <typename T>
  T maxVal(T a, T b){ return a > b ? a : b; }

  std::vector<int> v;

Rust:
  fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(list: &[T]) -> &T { /* ... */ }

Go (1.18+):
  func Max[T constraints.Ordered](a, b T) T { if a > b { return a }; return b }

Generics reduce duplication and keep type checking at compile time.

A lambda (anonymous function) is a small function defined inline without a name.

Python:
  square = lambda x: x * x
  print(square(5))           # 25
  nums = [1, 2, 3]
  doubled = list(map(lambda n: n * 2, nums))

JavaScript:
  const square = x => x * x;
  [1, 2, 3].map(n => n * 2);

Java:
  Function<Integer, Integer> square = x -> x * x;
  square.apply(5);

C++:
  auto square = [](int x){ return x * x; };
  std::cout << square(5);

Rust:
  let square = |x: i32| x * x;
  println!("{}", square(5));

Go:
  square := func(x int) int { return x * x }

Use lambdas for short, throwaway functions passed to map, filter, sort, event handlers.

A decorator wraps a function to extend its behavior without modifying it. Python has first-class support with @syntax.

Python:
  import time

  def timer(fn):
      def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
          start = time.time()
          result = fn(*args, **kwargs)
          print(f'{fn.__name__} took {time.time() - start:.3f}s')
          return result
      return wrapper

  @timer
  def slow():
      time.sleep(0.5)

  slow()

Built-in decorators:
  @staticmethod, @classmethod, @property
  @functools.lru_cache, @functools.wraps

TypeScript (experimental decorators):
  function log(target: any, key: string, desc: PropertyDescriptor) {
    const orig = desc.value;
    desc.value = function(...args: any[]){
      console.log(`calling ${key}`);
      return orig.apply(this, args);
    };
  }

  class A {
    @log
    greet(){ return 'hi'; }
  }

A generator produces a sequence of values lazily, one at a time, using 'yield'. It uses less memory than building a full list.

Python:
  def count_up_to(n):
      i = 1
      while i <= n:
          yield i
          i += 1

  for x in count_up_to(5):
      print(x)   # 1, 2, 3, 4, 5

  # Generator expression
  squares = (x*x for x in range(10))
  print(sum(squares))

JavaScript (generator function):
  function* countUpTo(n){
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) yield i;
  }
  for (const x of countUpTo(5)) console.log(x);

Advantages: lazy evaluation, constant memory, works with infinite sequences.

Use for: streaming data, reading huge files line by line, pipelines.

A switch (or match) selects one of many code paths based on the value of an expression.

C:
  switch (day) {
      case 1: printf("Mon"); break;
      case 2: printf("Tue"); break;
      default: printf("Other");
  }

Java:
  switch (day) {
      case 1 -> System.out.println("Mon");
      case 2 -> System.out.println("Tue");
      default -> System.out.println("Other");
  }

JavaScript:
  switch (day) {
    case 1: console.log('Mon'); break;
    case 2: console.log('Tue'); break;
    default: console.log('Other');
  }

Rust (match is exhaustive):
  match day {
      1 => println!("Mon"),
      2 => println!("Tue"),
      _ => println!("Other"),
  }

Python 3.10+ match:
  match day:
      case 1: print('Mon')
      case 2: print('Tue')
      case _: print('Other')

Go switch (no fallthrough unless explicit):
  switch day {
  case 1: fmt.Println("Mon")
  case 2: fmt.Println("Tue")
  default: fmt.Println("Other")
  }

A webhook is a user-defined HTTP callback: an external service sends an HTTP POST to your URL when an event happens. Instead of polling, the service pushes events to you.

Flow:
  1. You register a URL with the provider.
  2. An event happens (payment succeeds, PR merged, etc.).
  3. Provider sends POST with JSON payload to your URL.
  4. Your server verifies and processes it.

Example receiver (Node.js / Express):
  const express = require('express');
  const crypto = require('crypto');
  const app = express();

  app.post('/webhook', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), (req, res) => {
    const signature = req.headers['x-signature'];
    const expected = crypto.createHmac('sha256', SECRET)
      .update(req.body).digest('hex');
    if (signature !== expected) return res.status(400).end();

    const event = JSON.parse(req.body);
    handleEvent(event);
    res.status(200).end();
  });

Best practices: verify signatures, respond fast (200), process async, retry on failure, idempotency keys.

A service worker is a background script that runs separately from the web page. It can intercept network requests, cache assets, and enable offline functionality and push notifications.

Register (main.js):
  if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
    navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');
  }

Basic sw.js (cache-first):
  const CACHE = 'v1';
  const ASSETS = ['/', '/index.html', '/app.js', '/style.css'];

  self.addEventListener('install', event => {
    event.waitUntil(
      caches.open(CACHE).then(c => c.addAll(ASSETS))
    );
  });

  self.addEventListener('fetch', event => {
    event.respondWith(
      caches.match(event.request).then(r => r || fetch(event.request))
    );
  });

Notes:
  - Requires HTTPS or localhost.
  - Cannot access DOM directly.
  - Runs after page unload; can handle background sync and push.
  - Use 'skipWaiting' and 'clients.claim' for immediate activation.

A buffer overflow happens when a program writes more data into a fixed-size buffer than it can hold, overwriting adjacent memory. It is a common and dangerous vulnerability in C and C++.

Vulnerable C:
  void copy(char *input){
      char buf[10];
      strcpy(buf, input);   // unsafe if input > 9 chars
  }

Safe:
  void copy(char *input){
      char buf[10];
      snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s", input);
  }

Consequences:
  - Crash
  - Corrupted data
  - Arbitrary code execution
  - Privilege escalation

Mitigations:
  - Use safe functions (snprintf, strncpy, std::string)
  - Bounds-check before copying
  - Enable compiler protections: stack canaries, ASLR, DEP
  - Use languages with memory safety (Rust, Go, Java)
  - Static analysis tools

CWE-120, CWE-121 are the relevant vulnerability classes.

Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern where an object receives its dependencies from outside instead of creating them itself. It promotes loose coupling and testability.

Without DI:
  class UserService {
    private db = new MySQLDatabase();    // hard-coded
  }

With DI (constructor injection):
  class UserService {
    constructor(private db: Database) {}
  }

  const service = new UserService(new PostgresDatabase());

Python:
  class UserService:
      def __init__(self, db):
          self.db = db

  # In production
  UserService(PostgresDatabase())
  # In tests
  UserService(FakeDatabase())

Java (Spring):
  @Service
  class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;
    UserService(UserRepository repo){ this.repo = repo; }
  }

Benefits: testability (mock dependencies), flexibility (swap implementations), clearer dependencies.

DI frameworks: Spring (Java), Guice, Dagger, NestJS (TS), FastAPI Depends, .NET DI.

Both are test doubles used in unit testing, but they have different purposes.

Stub: returns canned answers, no assertions.
  class FakeUserRepo {
    getUser(id){ return { id, name: 'Test' }; }
  }

Mock: records interactions and verifies behavior (which methods were called, with what arguments).
  // Jest
  const repo = { getUser: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: 1 }) };
  const service = new UserService(repo);
  await service.getUser(1);
  expect(repo.getUser).toHaveBeenCalledWith(1);

Python (unittest.mock):
  from unittest.mock import Mock
  repo = Mock()
  repo.get_user.return_value = {'id': 1}
  service.get_user(1)
  repo.get_user.assert_called_once_with(1)

Other doubles:
  Fake: simplified working implementation (in-memory DB)
  Spy: wraps a real object and records calls
  Dummy: passed but never used (fills a required parameter)

Rule of thumb: use stubs for state verification, mocks for behavior verification.

A stress test pushes a system beyond normal operating conditions to see how it behaves under load, find limits, and identify failure modes.

Types:
  - Load test: expected peak traffic
  - Stress test: beyond peak, find breaking point
  - Spike test: sudden burst
  - Soak test: sustained load for hours (finds leaks)

Metrics to watch:
  - Throughput (requests/sec)
  - Latency (p50, p95, p99)
  - Error rate
  - CPU, memory, disk, network
  - Connection pool saturation
  - Queue depth

Tools:
  - k6 (JS scripting)
  - JMeter (GUI)
  - Locust (Python)
  - Gatling (Scala DSL)
  - wrk, hey, ab (simple)
  - Vegeta

k6 example:
  import http from 'k6/http';
  import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

  export const options = {
    vus: 100,
    duration: '30s',
  };

  export default function(){
    const res = http.get('https://api.example.com/users');
    check(res, { 'status 200': r => r.status === 200 });
    sleep(1);
  }

Run: k6 run script.js

A design token is a named value that represents a design decision, such as a color, spacing, font size, or shadow. Tokens keep design and code consistent across platforms.

Example (CSS variables):
  :root {
    --color-primary: #3563e9;
    --color-text: #12151c;
    --color-bg: #ffffff;
    --space-1: 4px;
    --space-2: 8px;
    --space-3: 16px;
    --radius-sm: 4px;
    --radius-md: 8px;
    --font-body: system-ui, sans-serif;
    --shadow-sm: 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.06);
  }

  .button {
    background: var(--color-primary);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }

Tools: Style Dictionary, Tokens Studio (Figma), Theo, Amazon Style Dictionary.

Benefits: single source of truth, easy theming, consistent design, works across web, iOS, Android, and backend.

Tree shaking removes unused code from a bundle. It relies on static analysis of ES module imports/exports to know what is actually used.

Example:
  // math.js
  export function add(a, b){ return a + b; }
  export function subtract(a, b){ return a - b; }
  export function multiply(a, b){ return a * b; }

  // main.js
  import { add } from './math.js';
  console.log(add(2, 3));

Bundler outputs only 'add'; subtract and multiply are shaken away.

Requirements:
  - ES modules (import/export) — CommonJS is harder to shake
  - Side-effect-free modules ('sideEffects: false' in package.json)
  - Production build with minification

Webpack, Rollup, Vite, esbuild all support tree shaking. In Webpack, mark pure modules:
  {
    "sideEffects": ["*.css"]
  }

Beware: modules with side effects (registering globals, patching prototypes) cannot be safely removed.

Lazy loading defers loading of resources until they are needed. It improves initial load time and reduces bandwidth.

Examples:

Images:
  <img src="placeholder.jpg" data-src="photo.jpg" loading="lazy" alt="">

Modern HTML:
  <img src="photo.jpg" loading="lazy" alt="">

JavaScript modules (dynamic import):
  button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const { heavyModule } = await import('./heavy.js');
    heavyModule.run();
  });

React lazy + Suspense:
  const Heavy = React.lazy(() => import('./Heavy'));

  <React.Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
    <Heavy />
  </React.Suspense>

Vue:
  const Heavy = () => import('./Heavy.vue');

Routes:
  Load route components only when the user navigates to them.

Benefits: smaller initial bundle, faster first paint, better performance on slow networks.

Watch: avoid delaying critical content; use placeholders to avoid layout shift.

A Web Worker runs JavaScript in a background thread, separate from the main UI thread. It can do heavy computation without blocking the UI.

Create a worker (main.js):
  const worker = new Worker('worker.js');

  worker.onmessage = e => {
    console.log('result:', e.data);
  };

  worker.postMessage({ cmd: 'sum', nums: [1, 2, 3, 4] });

Worker file (worker.js):
  self.onmessage = e => {
    const { cmd, nums } = e.data;
    if (cmd === 'sum'){
      const total = nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
      self.postMessage(total);
    }
  };

Inline worker:
  const blob = new Blob([code], { type: 'application/javascript' });
  const worker = new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob));

Limitations:
  - No direct DOM access
  - Communication via messages (structured clone)
  - Separate global scope (self)

Variants: SharedWorker (shared across tabs), ServiceWorker (network/offline).

Use for: data processing, parsing, image manipulation, cryptography, heavy calculations.

A memory cache stores frequently accessed data in RAM for fast retrieval. It trades memory for speed.

Examples:
  - Python: functools.lru_cache, dict
  - JavaScript: Map, WeakMap
  - Redis, Memcached (external)
  - HTTP caches (browser, CDN, reverse proxy)
  - CPU caches (L1, L2, L3)

Python LRU cache:
  from functools import lru_cache

  @lru_cache(maxsize=128)
  def fib(n):
      if n < 2: return n
      return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)

  fib(50)   # very fast because of memoization

JavaScript Map cache:
  const cache = new Map();

  function expensive(n){
    if (cache.has(n)) return cache.get(n);
    const result = /* compute */ n * n;
    cache.set(n, result);
    return result;
  }

WeakMap for object keys that can be garbage collected:
  const meta = new WeakMap();
  meta.set(obj, { seen: true });

Eviction policies: LRU, LFU, FIFO, TTL. Pick based on access pattern.

JSON Schema is a vocabulary for describing and validating the shape of JSON data. It defines required fields, types, patterns, ranges, and more.

Example schema:
  {
    "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
    "type": "object",
    "required": ["name", "email"],
    "properties": {
      "name": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1 },
      "email": { "type": "string", "format": "email" },
      "age": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 0, "maximum": 150 },
      "tags": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" } }
    },
    "additionalProperties": false
  }

Validation (JavaScript with Ajv):
  const Ajv = require('ajv');
  const ajv = new Ajv();
  const validate = ajv.compile(schema);
  const ok = validate(data);
  if (!ok) console.error(validate.errors);

Python (jsonschema):
  import jsonschema
  jsonschema.validate(instance=data, schema=schema)

Use for: API contracts, config validation, form validation, data pipelines, OpenAPI.

A transaction is a unit of work that either completes entirely or not at all. It groups multiple operations so they are atomic, consistent, isolated, and durable (ACID).

SQL example:
  BEGIN;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
  COMMIT;

If anything fails before COMMIT, use ROLLBACK.

Python:
  try:
      with conn:
          conn.execute('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1')
          conn.execute('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2')
  except Exception:
      # context manager rolls back on exception
      pass

Java:
  try (Connection c = dataSource.getConnection()){
      c.setAutoCommit(false);
      try (PreparedStatement p = c.prepareStatement(sql)){
          // ...
          c.commit();
      } catch (SQLException e){
          c.rollback();
          throw e;
      }
  }

Isolation levels: READ UNCOMMITTED, READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE. Higher isolation prevents more anomalies but reduces concurrency.

A stored procedure is a set of SQL statements stored on the database server and executed by name. It can take parameters, contain control flow, and return results.

PostgreSQL (PL/pgSQL):
  CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION add_user(p_name TEXT, p_email TEXT)
  RETURNS INTEGER AS $$
  DECLARE
      new_id INTEGER;
  BEGIN
      INSERT INTO users (name, email)
      VALUES (p_name, p_email)
      RETURNING id INTO new_id;
      RETURN new_id;
  END;
  $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

  SELECT add_user('Alice', 'alice@example.com');

MySQL:
  DELIMITER //
  CREATE PROCEDURE get_users_by_city(IN city VARCHAR(50))
  BEGIN
      SELECT * FROM users WHERE city = city;
  END //
  DELIMITER ;

  CALL get_users_by_city('Paris');

Pros: performance, encapsulation, security (grant EXECUTE only).
Cons: harder to version control, vendor-specific, business logic in DB.

Modern preference: keep business logic in application code; use procedures for pure performance or data integrity.
